  "query_preview": "..\nMostly cloudy skies today with some very light showers moving\nthrough the area. A deep trough continues to be the predominant\nupper-level pattern across the western US with the center of low\npressure now located over southern Nevada. Below normal\ntemperatures will continue through the week with high\ntemperatures 8-10 degrees below normal in the upper 70s to low\n80s. The high amplitude trough that has been the prevailing\nweather patten for the last several days will begin to move\neastward out of the area today and tomorrow. \n\nAs the trough moves out of the area a cut off low will extend \noff the main trough bringing a back door cold front to the \nsouthwest US. At the same time, surface high pressure will build\nin the southern Plains tightening the pressure gradients. This \ncontinues to show a strong signal for a significant easterly \nwind event starting as early as Thursday night lasting to \nSaturday morning. Current forecast has sustained winds of 30-40 \nmph with gusts 50-60+ mph. The strongest winds may be focused \nimmediately downstream of the higher terrain, terrain gaps and \neast-west oriented valleys will also be susceptible to strong \nwinds given the magnitude and duration of the low-level pressure\ngradient. Confidence continues to increase for a prolonged \nperiod of high winds across southern Arizona. Latest NBM \nprobabilities have a widespread area with 50-80% chance for \ngreater than 50 mph wind gusts from Friday morning through late \nFriday evening and a 30-50% chance for greater than 60 mph wind \ngusts.",
